# Autonomous issue agent — SHARED config TEMPLATE.
# Copy to config/shared.env (gitignored) and fill in. `bin/point-at.sh` sources
# config/shared.env FIRST, then overlays config/repos/<name>.env (per-repo wins).
# Never put a REPO/TARGET_DIR here — those live in the per-repo overlay.
#
#   cp config/shared.example.env config/shared.env   # then edit
#
# Repo-independent knobs only: GitHub App identity, identities, Jira, fixer runtime.

# ── GitHub App identity (review + steward run as the App bot) ──────────────────
# The reviewer + steward act as a GitHub App, NOT a PAT — GitHub rejects a review
# approval from the same identity that authored the PR (422 self-approval), and an
# App gives a clean second bot identity with short-lived (~hourly) auto-minted
# tokens instead of a long-lived PAT.
#
# One-time setup:
#   1. Create a GitHub App (org-level). Permissions: pull_requests:write,
#      issues:write, contents:read, metadata:read.
#   2. Generate + download its private key (.pem); point GH_APP_KEY_FILE at it.
#   3. Install the App on the target org/repo; grab the installation ID.
#   4. Fill the three values below. REVIEW_BOT/STEWARD_BOT default to
#      my-reviewer-bot[bot] — only set them if your App has a different name.
GH_APP_ID="<your-app-id>"
GH_APP_INSTALL_ID="<your-installation-id>"
GH_APP_KEY_FILE="$HOME/.config/<your-app>/private-key.pem"
# GH_APP_TOKEN_CACHE defaults to ~/.local/state/issue-agent-bot/app-token.json.

# ── Identities ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fixer = ambient `gh auth` login (YOUR github user or a PAT). review+steward = the
# App bot above. FIX_BOT MUST differ from REVIEW/STEWARD (the 422 self-approval
# rule); REVIEW and STEWARD may coincide (the steward never approves PRs).
# REVIEW_BOT/STEWARD_BOT are commented out — they default to the App login, so
# supplying only the App key + your FIX_BOT is enough. Uncomment to override.
IDENTITY_MODE="three"
FIX_BOT="<your-gh-login>"
# REVIEW_BOT="my-reviewer-bot[bot]"
# STEWARD_BOT="my-reviewer-bot[bot]"

# ── Jira sync (optional; steward mirrors GitHub issues into a Jira project) ─────
# INERT until JIRA_PROJECT_KEY is set. Leave JIRA_SYNC=0 to disable entirely.
JIRA_SYNC="0"
# JIRA_PROJECT_KEY="ACME"
# JIRA_CLOUD_ID="<your-atlassian-cloud-id>"   # e.g. yoursite.atlassian.net

# ── Steward triage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STEWARD_PROMOTE=0 → a human relabels ai-ready (a gate before the fixer runs).
# STEWARD_PROMOTE=1 → ready issues auto-relabel ai-triage→ai-ready (autonomous).
STEWARD_TRIAGE="1"
STEWARD_PROMOTE="0"

# ── Fixer runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "claude" = Claude Agent SDK (default, simplest). "codex" = OpenAI Codex CLI
# (needs `codex` on PATH + an OPENAI_API_KEY that authenticates to your gateway).
FIXER_RUNTIME="claude"
# For codex, point at your OpenAI-compatible gateway and model:
# OPENAI_API_KEY="${OPENAI_API_KEY:-}"
# OPENAI_BASE_URL="https://your-gateway/v1"
# CODEX_ENV_KEY="OPENAI_API_KEY"
# CODEX_BASE_URL="${OPENAI_BASE_URL}"
# CODEX_MODEL="<your-model-slug>"

# ── LLM credentials (Anthropic) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The reviewer + steward (and the claude-runtime fixer) drive Claude using YOUR
# Anthropic auth — the daemons inherit the launching shell's environment. Prefer
# exporting these in that shell (they're operator/machine-level, not repo config):
#   export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...                       # direct API key, OR
#   export ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=... ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://your-gateway
# If no ANTHROPIC_* env is set, the SDK falls back to your interactive `claude`
# login (~/.claude/.credentials.json). Either way it bills your own account/quota.
